		<h3 id="automation-server-label">Automation Server</h3>
		<h4>A Docker Server for a Non-Profit</h4>
		<p>A self-hosted Docker server for a 501(c)(3) non-profit that replaced a huge amount of manual data work. Before it existed, getting any real reporting done meant sorting and combining Excel files by hand, with no shared database, no real backups, and no way to connect one system's data to another. This server gives the organization a real database with real backups, plus the ability to connect nearly any two systems together, freeing up staff to focus on the mission instead of manual data work. It's still actively growing and, between the time it saves and the reporting it makes possible, it consistently saves 40+ hours of staff time a week, sometimes 80+ on busier weeks.</p>
		<p>It runs on a Dell server running Debian, with every service (routing, data storage, automation, and monitoring) in its own Docker container behind Traefik, which owns ports 80 and 443, handles TLS, and routes each incoming request to the right container by hostname.</p>
		<p>NocoDB is the primary data store, a spreadsheet-like interface over a real database with no practical row or column limit, connected to Cloudflare R2 for file storage (PDFs, images, etc.) and secured with Google SSO.</p>
		<p>n8n handles automation, triggered by NocoDB webhooks, schedules, and forms, and can genuinely connect anything to anything via http(s) requests. One workflow collects all sales data, filters and cleans the data, converts the result to a PDF, and sends it straight to a printer with no manual steps in between. There are over 65 workflows running today, each allowing employees to focus on value-add projects instead of repetitive tasks.</p>
		<p>Dozzle gives remote visibility into every container's logs.</p>
		<h4>Technologies</h4>
		<p>The server runs Debian on a Dell server, with Traefik as a reverse proxy and TLS terminator (using Let's Encrypt for certificates) in front of NocoDB, n8n, and Dozzle, all managed via Docker Compose.</p>
